---
// Makes a `<lite-youtube>` in a post's Markdown work — Paul Irish's facade
// element, which draws the poster frame and YouTube's play button as plain
// HTML and only builds the real <iframe> once someone clicks it.
//
// WHY A FACADE AND NOT AN <iframe>. A YouTube embed is about 1.3 MB of
// JavaScript and a dozen requests to five Google origins, all of it spent
// before anyone has decided to watch anything — on a page whose entire point
// is that the maths is rendered at build time and nothing but the palette
// switcher runs at all. The facade is 10 KB of JS and 3 KB of CSS, the poster
// is served from this origin (public/images/once-upon/), and the first request
// to Google is the one the click asks for. It embeds youtube-nocookie.com.
//
// WHY NOT astro-embed. Its <YouTube> component is a wrapper around exactly this
// package, and an .md file cannot call an Astro component anyway — only the
// element's own markup, which is what the post writes by hand. So the wrapper
// would buy nothing and the dependency would be a second copy of what is
// imported below. The one thing worth taking from it — an inline
// `background-image` for the poster, so the element never reaches for
// i.ytimg.com — is in the post's markup.
//
// WHY IT IS A COMPONENT AND NOT AN IMPORT IN Base.astro. Same bargain as
// MermaidRuntime beside it: a cost that belongs to a kind of content, charged
// to the routes that carry it. One post has a video; see the `hasYouTube`
// check in src/pages/[slug].astro.
//
// WITHOUT SCRIPT, or before this file has run, the element is its poster with
// a real <a> to the video over it — the "playbtn" in the markup is an anchor,
// which the element downgrades to a button once it takes over. So the failure
// mode is a link to YouTube, not a dead rectangle.
//
// THE STYLESHEET IS READ AS TEXT AND INLINED, which is the one thing here that
// is not the obvious spelling. A plain `import "…/lite-yt-embed.css"` sends the
// rules into the ROUTE's stylesheet — and [slug].astro is a single route that
// builds every post, so all eleven would carry the facade's CSS to pay for
// the one that shows a video. `?raw` + `is:inline` keeps Astro's bundler out of
// it: the 3 KB lands in this page's <head> as text, on the page that asked for
// it and nowhere else, and saves the request it would otherwise cost. The
// package stays the source of the bytes, so upgrading it upgrades this.
import css from "lite-youtube-embed/src/lite-yt-embed.css?raw";
---

<style is:inline set:html={css}></style>

<style is:inline>
  /* The package caps the facade at 720px and leaves it against the left edge;
     every other figure in these posts is centred in the measure.

     612px is that cap 15% narrower — a video that sits inside the 850px column
     rather than filling it, the same way the fork drawing is set at 60% of the
     measure. It is a MAX-width, so it changes the desktop and leaves the phone
     alone: below 612px the element is already the full column and stays there,
     which is where a video wants to be on a 390px screen. */
  lite-youtube {
    max-width: 612px;
    margin-inline: auto;
  }
</style>

<script>
  import "lite-youtube-embed";
</script>
